\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
**Example 1**
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us consider the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A =
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To verify that it is a Hermitian matrix, we can verify its conjugate transpose:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A^\dagger =
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}
= A.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Its eigenvalues are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
[1,\;1,\;2].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And the corresponding eigenvectors (each column is an eigenvector) are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have then that eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 is degenerate. We can reconstruct matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 by spectral decomposition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A =
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have now defined a Hermitian matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B =
\begin{pmatrix}
3 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 4 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can verify that they commute, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $AB-BA=0$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
AB-BA =
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the simplest case, we then have a subspace of only one dimension for eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

. We can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $Bv=bv$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $b=5$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
5
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now for eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, we have a two-dimensional subspace formed by the vectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All non-null vectors in this subspace continue to be eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with the same eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we denote the two eigenvectors by 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_1,v_2)$
\end_inset

 and use them as a basis, any non-null vector that is a linear combination of them,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
u = av_1+bv_2,
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

. In fact,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Au
=
A(av_1+bv_2)
=
aAv_1+bAv_2
=
av_1+bv_2
=
u.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to the first eigenvector, we can see that it is a linear combination of the other two vectors in the subspace and is not a 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 eigenvector:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
0\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
3\\
1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is, even though these two eigenvectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 are a basis for this subspace, they do not form a common eigenvector basis. But we can build one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since all non-null vectors in this subspace are eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we only need to find those who are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this two-dimensional space, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's action on the vectors of the space is given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B_1 =
\begin{pmatrix}
3 & 1\\
1 & 4
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Its eigenvalues are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
[2.38,\;4.62].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And the corresponding eigenvectors (each column is an eigenvector) are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85 & -0.53\\
0.53 & -0.85
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Combining with the previous results, we can have a new basis given by the eigenvectors (where each column corresponds to a vector of the new basis):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85 & -0.53 & 0\\
0.53 & -0.85 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can test by applying 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to the vectors of the new basis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
=
2 \begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the corresponding eigenvalues:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
[1,\;1,\;2].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can test by applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to the vectors of the new basis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
2.38
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
4.62
\begin{pmatrix}
-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
=
5
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the corresponding eigenvalues:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
[2.38,\;4.62,\;5].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
**Example 2**
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we have the operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can verify that they do not commute, since
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
[A,B]=AB-BA
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & -2\\
2 & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\neq 0.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the operator 
\begin_inset Formula \(A\)
\end_inset

, the eigenvalues are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
a=+1,\,-1,
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with corresponding normalized eigenvectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
|a=+1\rangle
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
|a=-1\rangle
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
-1\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose, for example, that after measuring 
\begin_inset Formula \(A\)
\end_inset

, we obtain the value 
\begin_inset Formula \(a=+1\)
\end_inset

. The system is then in the corresponding eigenstate,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
|\psi\rangle
=
|a=+1\rangle
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now consider measuring 
\begin_inset Formula \(B\)
\end_inset

. The eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula \(B\)
\end_inset

 are also
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
b=+1,\,-1,
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with corresponding eigenvectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
|b=+1\rangle
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
|b=-1\rangle
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The probability of obtaining a particular eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula \(b\)
\end_inset

 is given by the squared magnitude of the inner product between the corresponding eigenvector and the state of the system:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P(b)=|\langle b|\psi\rangle|^2.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P(b=+1)
=
|\langle b=+1|a=+1\rangle|^2
=
\frac{1}{2},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P(b=-1)
=
|\langle b=-1|a=+1\rangle|^2
=
\frac{1}{2}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, there is a 
\begin_inset Formula \(50\%\)
\end_inset

 probability of observing 
\begin_inset Formula \(+1\)
\end_inset

 and a 
\begin_inset Formula \(50\%\)
\end_inset

 probability of observing 
\begin_inset Formula \(-1\)
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words, since 
\begin_inset Formula \(A\)
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \(B\)
\end_inset

 do not commute and, in this example, do not share any eigenvectors, after measuring one of the observables, the outcome of a subsequent measurement of the other is intrinsically indeterminate within this mathematical formalism.
\end_layout

